---
on:
  issues:
    types: [opened]

engine: copilot

permissions:
  contents: read
  issues: read
  actions: read

runs-on: [self-hosted, linux, x64]
runs-on-slim: [self-hosted, linux, x64]

tools:
  github:
    toolsets: [default, projects]
    github-app:
      client-id: ${{ vars.APP_CLIENT_ID }}
      private-key: ${{ secrets.APP_PRIVATE_KEY }}
      owner: "YOUR-ORG"
      repositories: ["*"]

safe-outputs:
  github-app:
    client-id: ${{ vars.APP_CLIENT_ID }}
    private-key: ${{ secrets.APP_PRIVATE_KEY }}
    owner: "YOUR-ORG"
  update-project:
    project: "https://github.com/orgs/YOUR-ORG/projects/1"
    max: 1
  add-comment:
    max: 1
---

# Project Triage

A new issue was opened. Read it, then add it to the project board and set its fields.

Always include the full project URL in the `update_project` call:
`https://github.com/orgs/YOUR-ORG/projects/1`

Set fields only from these exact values — never invent new ones:

- **Status**: `Needs Triage` | `Ready` | `In Progress` | `Blocked`
- **Priority**: `P0` | `P1` | `P2`
- **Team**: `Platform` | `Infra` | `Tooling`

New issues start at `Needs Triage` unless the report is complete enough to be
actionable, in which case use `Ready`. Reserve `P0` for production impact.

Then post one short comment explaining the routing and flagging any uncertainty.

If for some reason no action is appropriate, you MUST call the `noop` tool:
`{"noop": {"message": "No action needed: [reason]"}}`
